654971Z -               '* Ordering comparison methods, `a < b` (`__lt__`): Raise a `TypeError` if the objects cannot be ordered.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6655650Z -               '* Most others: If the operation is never supported, the method often does not need to be implemented at all; otherwise a `TypeError` should be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6655741Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6655868Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6656916Z +               'Replace all calls to `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` that do not provide a custom failure message with a more specific variant. Alternatively, provide a tailored failure message using the `assertTrue(condition, message)` form.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6658168Z -               'If the method always raises as exception, then if it is intended to be an abstract method, the `@abstractmethod` decorator should be used. Otherwise, ensure that the method raises an exception of the correct type, or remove the method if the operation does not need to be supported.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6658255Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6658338Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6658449Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6658736Z +               'In this example, `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` are used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6659924Z -               'In the following example, the `__add__` method of `A` raises a `TypeError` if `other` is of the wrong type. However, it should return `NotImplemented` instead of rising an exception, to allow other classes to support adding to `A`. This is demonstrated in the class `B`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660090Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660175Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660289Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660459Z +               'from unittest import TestCase\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660585Z -               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660747Z -               '    def __init__(self, a):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660884Z -               '        self.a = a \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6660969Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6661129Z +               'class MyTest(TestCase):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6661409Z -               '    def __add__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6661686Z -               '        # BAD: Should return NotImplemented instead of raising\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6661871Z -               '        if not isinstance(other,A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662145Z -               '            raise TypeError(f"Cannot add A to {other.__class__}")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662329Z -               '        return A(self.a + other.a)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662420Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662545Z -               'class B:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662702Z -               '    def __init__(self, a):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662834Z -               '        self.a = a \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6662932Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6663094Z -               '    def __add__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6663768Z -               '        # GOOD: Returning NotImplemented allows for the operation to fallback to other implementations to allow other classes to support adding to B.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6664089Z -               '        if not isinstance(other,B):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6664265Z -               '            return NotImplemented\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6664531Z -               '        return B(self.a + other.a)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6664626Z                 '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6664723Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6664885Z +               '    def testInts(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665065Z +               '        self.assertTrue(1 == 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665229Z +               '        self.assertFalse(1 > 2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665454Z +               '        self.assertTrue(1 in []) #This will fail\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665543Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665657Z -               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665753Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6665835Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6666662Z +               'This will make it more difficult to determine what has gone wrong when `self.assertTrue(1 in [])` fails. The failure message "AssertionError: False is not true" is not very helpful.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6667263Z -               'In the following example, the `__getitem__` method of `C` raises a `ValueError`, rather than a `KeyError` or `IndexError` as expected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6667348Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6667910Z +               'A more useful error message can be generated by changing the asserts to the more specific forms as in the following example.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6667994Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6668085Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6668197Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6668366Z +               'from unittest import TestCase\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6668485Z -               'class C:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6668701Z -               '    def __getitem__(self, idx):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6668862Z -               '        if self.idx < 0:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6669128Z -               '            # BAD: Should raise a KeyError or IndexError instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6669337Z -               '            raise ValueError("Invalid index")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6669509Z -               '        return self.lookup(idx)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6669681Z -               ' \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6669767Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6669919Z +               'class MyTest(TestCase):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670021Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670121Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670269Z +               '    def testInts(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670442Z +               '        self.assertEqual(1, 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670623Z +               '        self.assertLessEqual(1, 2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670823Z +               '        self.assertIn(1, []) #This will fail\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6670919Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6671161Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6671622Z +               'In this case, the failure message "AssertionError: 1 not found in \\[\\]" is much more informative.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6672833Z -               'In the following example, the class `__hash__` method of `D` raises `NotImplementedError`. This causes `D` to be incorrectly identified as hashable by `isinstance(obj, collections.abc.Hashable)`; so the correct way to make a class unhashable is to set `__hash__ = None`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6672923Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6673003Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6673129Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6673247Z -               'class D:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6673400Z -               '    def __hash__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6673606Z -               '        # BAD: Use `__hash__ = None` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6674038Z -               '        raise NotImplementedError(f"{self.__class__} is unhashable.")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6674144Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6674239Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6674351Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6675046Z +               '* Python library reference: [TestCase.assertEqual](https://docs.python.org/library/unittest.html#unittest.TestCase.assertEqual).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6675208Z +             text: '# Imprecise assert\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6676962Z +               'The class `unittest.TestCase` provides a range of assertion methods. As well as the general forms `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` more specific forms such as `assertGreaterEquals()` and `assertNotIn()` are provided. By using the more specific forms it is possible to get more precise and informative failure messages in the event of a test failing. This can speed up the debugging process.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6677562Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [Special Method Names](http://docs.python.org/dev/reference/datamodel.html#special-method-names).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6678001Z -               '* Python Library Reference: [Exceptions](https://docs.python.org/3/library/exceptions.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6678254Z -             text: '# Non-standard exception raised in special method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6680000Z -               'User-defined classes interact with the Python virtual machine via special methods (also called "magic methods"). For example, for a class to support addition it must implement the `__add__` and `__radd__` special methods. When the expression `a + b` is evaluated, the Python virtual machine will call `type(a).__add__(a, b)`, and if that is not implemented it will call `type(b).__radd__(b, a)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6680088Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6682036Z -               'Since the virtual machine calls these special methods for common expressions, users of the class will expect these operations to raise standard exceptions. For example, users would expect that the expression `a.b` may raise an `AttributeError` if the object `a` does not have an attribute `b`. If a `KeyError` were raised instead, then this would be unexpected and may break code that expected an `AttributeError`, but not a `KeyError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6682118Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6682768Z -               'Therefore, if a method is unable to perform the expected operation then its response should conform to the standard protocol, described below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6682935Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6683231Z -               '* Attribute access, `a.b` (`__getattr__`): Raise `AttributeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6683690Z -               '* Arithmetic operations, `a + b` (`__add__`): Do not raise an exception, return `NotImplemented` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6684099Z -               '* Indexing, `a[b]` (`__getitem__`): Raise `KeyError` or `IndexError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6684926Z -               '* Hashing, `hash(a)` (`__hash__`): Should not raise an exception. Use `__hash__ = None` to indicate that an object is unhashable rather than raising an exception.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6685357Z -               '* Equality methods, `a == b` (`__eq__`): Never raise an exception, always return `True` or `False`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6685827Z -               '* Ordering comparison methods, `a < b` (`__lt__`): Raise a `TypeError` if the objects cannot be ordered.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6686499Z -               '* Most others: If the operation is never supported, the method often does not need to be implemented at all; otherwise a `TypeError` should be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6686595Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6686723Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6687763Z +               'Replace all calls to `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` that do not provide a custom failure message with a more specific variant. Alternatively, provide a tailored failure message using the `assertTrue(condition, message)` form.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6689022Z -               'If the method always raises as exception, then if it is intended to be an abstract method, the `@abstractmethod` decorator should be used. Otherwise, ensure that the method raises an exception of the correct type, or remove the method if the operation does not need to be supported.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6689177Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6689264Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6689382Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6689668Z +               'In this example, `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` are used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6690849Z -               'In the following example, the `__add__` method of `A` raises a `TypeError` if `other` is of the wrong type. However, it should return `NotImplemented` instead of rising an exception, to allow other classes to support adding to `A`. This is demonstrated in the class `B`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6690941Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6691031Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6691142Z                 '```python\n' +
