2026-10-20T21:06:14.6751795Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6752492Z +             text: 'Using the iteration variable of a list comprehension in the enclosing scope will result in different behavior between Python 2 and 3 and is confusing.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6752963Z -             text: '`__del__` methods may be called at arbitrary times, perhaps never called at all, and should be simple.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6753040Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6753127Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6753423Z +             markdown: '# List comprehension variable used in enclosing scope\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6754716Z +               'In Python 2 list comprehensions are evaluated in the enclosing scope, which means that the iteration variable of a list comprehension is visible outside of the list comprehension. In Python 3 the iteration variable is no longer visible in the enclosing scope.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6754931Z -             markdown: '# Overly complex `__del__` method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6755854Z -               'The `__del__` method exists to release any resources held by an object when that object is deleted. The `__del__` is called only by the garbage collector which may call it after an indefinite delay or never.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6755942Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6756690Z +               'Code that uses the value of a list comprehension iteration variable after the list comprehension has finished will behave differently under Python 2 and Python 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6757400Z -               'Consequently, `__del__` method should not be relied on to release resources, such as file descriptors. Rather, these resources should be released explicitly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6757487Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6758083Z -               'The existence of a complex `__del__` method suggests that this is the main or only way to release resources associated with the object.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6758169Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6758261Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6758385Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6759172Z +               'Explicitly set the variable in the outer scope to the value that it would have held when run under Python 2. Then rename the list comprehension variable for additional clarity.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6759865Z -               'In order to ensure correct cleanup of the object add an explicit close(), or similar, method. Possibly make the object a context manager.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6759952Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6760167Z -               'The __del__ method should just call close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6760248Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6760344Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6760456Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6761729Z +               'In this example, `x` is initially assigned the value of 3. In Python 3, `x` will be unchanged as the list comprehension is evaluated in its own scope. In Python 2, evaluation of the list comprehension occurs in the scope of `two_or_three`, setting `x` to 2.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6762547Z -               'The first example below shows a class which relies on `__del__` to release resources. The second example shows an improved version of the class where `__del__` simply calls close.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6762632Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6762710Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6762822Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6762904Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6763045Z +               'def two_or_three():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6763174Z +               '    x = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6763336Z +               '    [0 for x in range(3)]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6763604Z +               '    return x # Will return 2 in Python 2 and 3 in Python 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6763989Z -               '#Relies on __del__ being called by the garbage collector.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6764245Z -               'class CachedPreferencesFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6764336Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6764492Z +               'print(two_or_three())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6764589Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6765198Z +               'The following example is the same code as above, but the list comprehension variable is renamed to ensure it does not overwrite `x`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6765306Z -               '    ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6765387Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6765540Z -               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6765785Z -               '        for key, value in self.preferences.items():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6765974Z -               '            self.write_pair(key, value)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766151Z -               '        self.backing.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766237Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766357Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766446Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766580Z +               'def just_three():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766703Z +               '    x = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6766860Z +               '    [0 for y in range(3)]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767057Z +               '    return x # Will return always return 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767197Z -               '#Better version\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767364Z -               'class CachedPreferencesFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767444Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767588Z +               'print(just_three())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767683Z -               '    ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767783Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6767933Z -               '    def close(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6768162Z -               '        for key, value in self.preferences.items():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6768352Z -               '            self.write_pair(key, value)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6768514Z -               '        self.backing.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6768606Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6768789Z -               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6769010Z -               '        self.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6769100Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6769187Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6769265Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6769384Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6769935Z +               '* Python Tutorial: [List Comprehensions](https://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/datastructures.html#list-comprehensions).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6770699Z +               '* The History of Python: [From List Comprehensions to Generator Expressions](http://python-history.blogspot.co.uk/2010/06/from-list-comprehensions-to-generator.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6771341Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [List displays](https://docs.python.org/2/reference/expressions.html#list-displays).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6771624Z +             text: '# List comprehension variable used in enclosing scope\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6772766Z +               'In Python 2 list comprehensions are evaluated in the enclosing scope, which means that the iteration variable of a list comprehension is visible outside of the list comprehension. In Python 3 the iteration variable is no longer visible in the enclosing scope.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6773292Z -               '* Python Standard Library: [Context manager](http://docs.python.org/library/stdtypes.html#context-manager-types).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6773490Z -             text: '# Overly complex `__del__` method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6774493Z -               'The `__del__` method exists to release any resources held by an object when that object is deleted. The `__del__` is called only by the garbage collector which may call it after an indefinite delay or never.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6774582Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6775305Z +               'Code that uses the value of a list comprehension iteration variable after the list comprehension has finished will behave differently under Python 2 and Python 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6776100Z -               'Consequently, `__del__` method should not be relied on to release resources, such as file descriptors. Rather, these resources should be released explicitly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6776184Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6776784Z -               'The existence of a complex `__del__` method suggests that this is the main or only way to release resources associated with the object.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6776869Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6776962Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6777089Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6777871Z +               'Explicitly set the variable in the outer scope to the value that it would have held when run under Python 2. Then rename the list comprehension variable for additional clarity.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6778492Z -               'In order to ensure correct cleanup of the object add an explicit close(), or similar, method. Possibly make the object a context manager.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6778581Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6778791Z -               'The __del__ method should just call close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6778878Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6778976Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6779086Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6780213Z +               'In this example, `x` is initially assigned the value of 3. In Python 3, `x` will be unchanged as the list comprehension is evaluated in its own scope. In Python 2, evaluation of the list comprehension occurs in the scope of `two_or_three`, setting `x` to 2.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781023Z -               'The first example below shows a class which relies on `__del__` to release resources. The second example shows an improved version of the class where `__del__` simply calls close.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781105Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781192Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781308Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781460Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781612Z +               'def two_or_three():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781735Z +               '    x = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6781889Z +               '    [0 for x in range(3)]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6782161Z +               '    return x # Will return 2 in Python 2 and 3 in Python 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6782428Z -               '#Relies on __del__ being called by the garbage collector.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6782598Z -               'class CachedPreferencesFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6782687Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6782835Z +               'print(two_or_three())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6783053Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6783661Z +               'The following example is the same code as above, but the list comprehension variable is renamed to ensure it does not overwrite `x`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6783765Z -               '    ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6783969Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6784127Z -               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6784359Z -               '        for key, value in self.preferences.items():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6784554Z -               '            self.write_pair(key, value)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6784727Z -               '        self.backing.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6784808Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6784935Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6785016Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6785160Z +               'def just_three():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6785281Z +               '    x = 3\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6785436Z +               '    [0 for y in range(3)]\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6785646Z +               '    return x # Will return always return 3.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6785790Z -               '#Better version\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786038Z -               'class CachedPreferencesFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786128Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786279Z +               'print(just_three())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786378Z -               '    ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786475Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786615Z -               '    def close(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6786844Z -               '        for key, value in self.preferences.items():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787032Z -               '            self.write_pair(key, value)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787190Z -               '        self.backing.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787284Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787429Z -               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787568Z -               '        self.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787665Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787746Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6787832Z                 '\n' +
