2026-10-20T21:06:14.6797615Z +             markdown: '# Use of an undefined placeholder variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6798227Z +               'This place-holder variable may not be defined. If this code is executed and the variable is undefined then a `NameError` will occur.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6798464Z -             markdown: '# Modification of parameter with default\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6800558Z -               'The default value of a parameter is computed once when the function is created, not for every invocation. The "pre-computed" value is then used for every subsequent call to the function. Consequently, if you modify the default value for a parameter this "modified" default value is used for the parameter in future calls to the function. This means that the function may not behave as expected in future calls and also makes the function more difficult to understand.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6800654Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6800743Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6800868Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6801851Z +               'Check that the name of the place-holder variable is not a typographic error. If the name is correct, either define a value for the variable, or import the module that defines the function or method that sets the value.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6803462Z -               'If a parameter has a default value, do not modify the default value. When you use a mutable object as a default value, you should use a placeholder value instead of modifying the default value. This is a particular problem when you work with lists and dictionaries but there are standard methods of avoiding modifying the default parameter (see References).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6803619Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6804768Z +               'If another module initializes this variable before it is used, then the `NameError` may not occur. However, you can make the code more robust and clearer by setting the variable to a default value in its own module.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6804862Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6804989Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6806278Z -               'In the following example, the `default` parameter is set with a default value of an empty list. Other commands in the function then append values to the list. The next time the function is called, the list will contain values, which may not have been intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6806373Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6806468Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6806601Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6806702Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6807000Z -               '    def __init__(self, name, choices=[], default=[], shortDesc=None,\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6807418Z -               "                 longDesc=None, hints=None, allowNone=1):   # 'default' parameter assigned a value\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6807592Z -               '        self.choices = choices\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6807771Z -               '        if choices and not default:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6808174Z -               "            default.append(choices[0][1])                   # value of 'default' parameter modified\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6808594Z -               '        Argument.__init__(self, name, default, shortDesc, longDesc, hints, allowNone=allowNone)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6808760Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6809614Z -               'The recommended workaround is use a placeholder value. That is, define the function with a default of `default=None`, check if the parameter is `None` and then set the parameter to a list.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6809714Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6809804Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6809923Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6810437Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [NameError](https://docs.python.org/library/exceptions.html#exceptions.NameError).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6810670Z +             text: '# Use of an undefined placeholder variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6811277Z +               'This place-holder variable may not be defined. If this code is executed and the variable is undefined then a `NameError` will occur.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6811901Z -               '* Effbot: [Default Parameter Values in Python](https://web.archive.org/web/20201112004749/http://effbot.org/zone/default-values.htm).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6812514Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [Function definitions](http://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#function-definitions).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6812741Z -             text: '# Modification of parameter with default\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6814968Z -               'The default value of a parameter is computed once when the function is created, not for every invocation. The "pre-computed" value is then used for every subsequent call to the function. Consequently, if you modify the default value for a parameter this "modified" default value is used for the parameter in future calls to the function. This means that the function may not behave as expected in future calls and also makes the function more difficult to understand.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6815054Z                 '\n' +
