2026-10-20T21:06:14.6831347Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6831787Z +             text: 'Assignment to a variable occurs multiple times without any intermediate use of that variable'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6832537Z -             text: 'Overriding a method without ensuring that both methods accept the same number and type of parameters has the potential to cause an error when there is a mismatch.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6832683Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6832770Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6832983Z +             markdown: '# Variable defined multiple times\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6833571Z +               'Multiple assignments to a single variable without an intervening usage makes the first assignment redundant. Its value is lost.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6833937Z -             markdown: '# Signature mismatch in overriding method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6836001Z -               "When the signature of a method of a base class and a method of a subclass that overrides it don't match, a call to the base class method may not be a valid call to the subclass method, and thus raise an exception if an instance of the subclass is passed instead. If following the Liskov Substitution Principle, in which an instance of a subclass should be usable in every context as though it were an instance of the base class, this behavior breaks the principle.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6836088Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6836178Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6836304Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6836998Z +               'Ensure that the second assignment is in fact correct. Then delete the first assignment (taking care not to delete right hand side if it has side effects).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6837449Z -               'Ensure that the overriding method in the subclass accepts the same parameters as the base method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6837534Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6837615Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6837728Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6838364Z +               'In this example, `x` is assigned the value of 42 but then the value is changed to 12 before `x` is used. This makes the first assignment useless.\n' +
