2026-10-20T21:06:14.6861620Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6861982Z +             text: 'Using a local variable before it is initialized causes an UnboundLocalError.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6862342Z -             text: 'Explicitly returning a value from an `__init__` method will raise a TypeError.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6862427Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6862515Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6862888Z +             markdown: '# Potentially uninitialized local variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6864189Z +               'This local variable may be used before it is defined. If a variable is assigned to in a function and not explicitly declared `global` or `nonlocal` then it is assumed to be a local variable. If it is used before it is defined then an `UnboundLocalError` will be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6864419Z -             markdown: '# `__init__` method returns a value\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6865602Z -               'The `__init__` method of a class is used to initialize new objects, not create them. As such, it should not return any value. Returning `None` is correct in the sense that no runtime error will occur, but it suggests that the returned value is meaningful, which it is not.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6865693Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6865779Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6865898Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6867503Z +               'Review the code and consider the intended scope of the variable. Determine whether the variable should be global or local in scope. If a global variable is required then add a `global` statement, or in Python 3 you can use a `nonlocal` statement if the variable occurs in an enclosing function. Otherwise, ensure that the variable is defined before it is used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6868142Z -               'Convert the `return expr` statement to a plain `return` statement, or omit it altogether if it is at the end of the method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6868224Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6868310Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6868427Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6869272Z +               'The following code includes different functions that use variables. `test1()` fails with an `UnboundLocalError` because the local variable `var` is used before it is initialized.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6869911Z -               'In this example, the `__init__` method attempts to return the newly created object. This is an error and the return method should be removed.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6869999Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870078Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870193Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870320Z +               'def test():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870453Z +               '    var = 1 \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870607Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870841Z +               '        print var      # Use variable from outer scope\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6870981Z +               '    print_var()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871115Z +               '    print var \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871205Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871301Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871432Z +               'def test1():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871553Z +               '    var = 2 \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871702Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6871992Z +               '        print var       # Attempt to use variable from local scope. \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6872289Z +               '        var = 3         # Since this is not initialized yet, this results\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6872521Z +               '    print_var()         # in an UnboundLocalError\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6872727Z +               '    print var \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6872830Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6872928Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6873055Z +               'def test2():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6873181Z +               '    var = 2 \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6873328Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6873586Z +               '        var = 3         # Initialize local version of the variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6873948Z +               '        print var       # Use variable from local scope.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6874308Z +               '    print_var()         # Note that this local variable "shadows" the variable from\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6874805Z +               '    print var           # outer scope which makes code more difficult to interpret.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6874914Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875009Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875145Z +               'def test3():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875276Z +               '    var = 4\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875422Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875713Z +               '        nonlocal var    # Use non-local variable from outer scope.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875853Z +               '        print var\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6875994Z +               '    print_var()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876130Z +               '    print var\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876323Z -               'class ExplicitReturnInInit(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876489Z -               '    def __init__(self, i):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876631Z -               '        self.i = i\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876772Z -               '        return self\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876861Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6876945Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6877129Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6877843Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [Built-in Exceptions: UnboundLocalError](http://docs.python.org/library/exceptions.html#exceptions.UnboundLocalError).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6878922Z +               '* Python Frequently Asked Questions: [Why am I getting an UnboundLocalError when the variable has a value?](http://docs.python.org/2/faq/programming.html#why-am-i-getting-an-unboundlocalerror-when-the-variable-has-a-value).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6879413Z +               '* Python Course: [Global and Local Variables](http://www.python-course.eu/global_vs_local_variables.php).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6880372Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The global statement](http://docs.python.org/2.7/reference/simple_stmts.html#index-54), [The nonlocal statement](http://docs.python.org/3.3/reference/simple_stmts.html#index-43).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6880607Z +             text: '# Potentially uninitialized local variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6881783Z +               'This local variable may be used before it is defined. If a variable is assigned to in a function and not explicitly declared `global` or `nonlocal` then it is assumed to be a local variable. If it is used before it is defined then an `UnboundLocalError` will be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6882247Z -               '* Python: [The __init__ method](http://docs.python.org/2.7/reference/datamodel.html#object.__init__).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6882450Z -             text: '# `__init__` method returns a value\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6883645Z -               'The `__init__` method of a class is used to initialize new objects, not create them. As such, it should not return any value. Returning `None` is correct in the sense that no runtime error will occur, but it suggests that the returned value is meaningful, which it is not.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6883726Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6883927Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6884054Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6885660Z +               'Review the code and consider the intended scope of the variable. Determine whether the variable should be global or local in scope. If a global variable is required then add a `global` statement, or in Python 3 you can use a `nonlocal` statement if the variable occurs in an enclosing function. Otherwise, ensure that the variable is defined before it is used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6886306Z -               'Convert the `return expr` statement to a plain `return` statement, or omit it altogether if it is at the end of the method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6886393Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6886472Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6886588Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6887519Z +               'The following code includes different functions that use variables. `test1()` fails with an `UnboundLocalError` because the local variable `var` is used before it is initialized.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888150Z -               'In this example, the `__init__` method attempts to return the newly created object. This is an error and the return method should be removed.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888239Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888323Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888432Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888565Z +               'def test():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888689Z +               '    var = 1 \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6888838Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889078Z +               '        print var      # Use variable from outer scope\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889211Z +               '    print_var()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889344Z +               '    print var \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889442Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889536Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889666Z +               'def test1():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6889787Z +               '    var = 2 \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6890004Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6890296Z +               '        print var       # Attempt to use variable from local scope. \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6890589Z +               '        var = 3         # Since this is not initialized yet, this results\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6890819Z +               '    print_var()         # in an UnboundLocalError\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6890956Z +               '    print var \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6891047Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6891141Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6891272Z +               'def test2():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6891394Z +               '    var = 2 \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6891540Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6891807Z +               '        var = 3         # Initialize local version of the variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6892052Z +               '        print var       # Use variable from local scope.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6892405Z +               '    print_var()         # Note that this local variable "shadows" the variable from\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6892747Z +               '    print var           # outer scope which makes code more difficult to interpret.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6892844Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6892940Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6893066Z +               'def test3():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6893196Z +               '    var = 4\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6893344Z +               '    def print_var():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6893624Z +               '        nonlocal var    # Use non-local variable from outer scope.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6893762Z +               '        print var\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894021Z +               '    print_var()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894154Z +               '    print var\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894350Z -               'class ExplicitReturnInInit(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894509Z -               '    def __init__(self, i):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894652Z -               '        self.i = i\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894794Z -               '        return self\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6894960Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6895046Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6895162Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6895867Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [Built-in Exceptions: UnboundLocalError](http://docs.python.org/library/exceptions.html#exceptions.UnboundLocalError).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6896943Z +               '* Python Frequently Asked Questions: [Why am I getting an UnboundLocalError when the variable has a value?](http://docs.python.org/2/faq/programming.html#why-am-i-getting-an-unboundlocalerror-when-the-variable-has-a-value).\n' +
