2026-10-20T21:06:14.6903974Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6904490Z +             text: 'Capturing a loop variable is not the same as capturing its value, and can lead to unexpected behavior or bugs.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6904690Z -             text: 'Special method has incorrect signature'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6904772Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6904862Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6905038Z +             markdown: '# Loop variable capture\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6906108Z +               'In Python, a nested function or lambda expression that captures a variable from its surrounding scope is a *late-binding* closure, meaning that the value of the variable is determined when the closure is called, not when it is created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6906437Z -             markdown: '# Special method has incorrect signature\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6908221Z -               'Special methods (sometimes also called magic methods) are how user defined classes interact with the Python virtual machine. For example, for a class to support addition it must implement the `__add__` and `__radd__` special methods. When the expression `a + b` is evaluated the Python virtual machine will call `type(a).__add__(a, b)` and if that is not implemented it will call `type(b).__radd__(b, a)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6908311Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6909518Z +               'Care must be taken when the captured variable is a loop variable. If the closure is called after the loop ends, it will use the value of the variable on the last iteration of the loop, rather than the value at the iteration at which it was created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6910497Z -               'Since these special methods are always called by the virtual machine with a fixed number of parameters, if the method is implemented with a different number of parameters it will fail at runtime with a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6910589Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6910674Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6910794Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6911817Z +               "Ensure that closures that capture loop variables aren't used outside of a single iteration of the loop. To capture the value of a loop variable at the time the closure is created, use a default parameter, or `functools.partial`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6912102Z -               'Ensure that the method has the correct number of parameters\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6912183Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6912335Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6912453Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6913064Z +               'In the following (BAD) example, a `tasks` list is created, but each task captures the loop variable `i`, and reads the same value when run.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6913746Z -               'In the example the `__str__` method has an extra parameter. This means that if `str(p)` is called when `p` is a `Point` then it will fail with a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6913943Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914030Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914142Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914349Z +               '# BAD: The loop variable `i` is captured.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914471Z +               'tasks = []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914622Z +               'for i in range(5):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914809Z +               '    tasks.append(lambda: print(i))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6914962Z -               '#-*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6915049Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6915364Z +               '# This will print `4,4,4,4,4`, rather than `0,1,2,3,4` as likely intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6915509Z +               'for t in tasks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6915632Z +               '    t() \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6915727Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6916718Z +               'In the following (GOOD) example, each closure has an `i` default parameter, shadowing the outer `i` variable, the default value of which is determined as the value of the loop variable `i` at the time the closure is created.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6916870Z -               'class Point(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6916950Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6917124Z -               '    def __init__(self, x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6917258Z -               '        self.x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6917385Z -               '        self.y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6917471Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6917591Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6917951Z +               '# GOOD: A default parameter is used, so the variable `i` is not being captured.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6918159Z +               'tasks = []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6918298Z +               'for i in range(5):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6918497Z +               '    tasks.append(lambda i=i: print(i))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6918665Z -               '    def __add__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6918857Z -               '        if not isinstance(other, Point):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6919038Z -               '            return NotImplemented\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6919291Z -               '        return Point(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6919490Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6919670Z +               '# This will print `0,1,2,3,4``.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6919803Z +               'for t in tasks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6919928Z +               '    t() \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6920029Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6920613Z +               'In the following (GOOD) example, `functools.partial` is used to partially evaluate the lambda expression with the value of `i`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6920871Z -               '    def __str__(self, style): #Spurious extra parameter\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6921039Z -               "        if style == 'polar':\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6921270Z -               '            u"%s @ %s\\u00b0" % (abs(self), self.angle())\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6921401Z -               '        else:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6921613Z -               '            return "[%s, %s]" % (self.x, self.y)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6924354Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6924599Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6924828Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6925048Z +               'import functools\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6925564Z +               '# GOOD: `functools.partial` takes care of capturing the _value_ of `i`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6925949Z +               'tasks = []\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6926203Z +               'for i in range(5):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6926689Z +               '    tasks.append(functools.partial(lambda i: print(i), i))\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6926847Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6927125Z +               '# This will print `0,1,2,3,4``.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6927351Z +               'for t in tasks:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6927546Z +               '    t() \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6927683Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.6927817Z                 '\n' +
