2026-10-20T21:06:14.7078006Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7078089Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7078299Z +             markdown: "# Use of 'global' at module level\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7079501Z +               'The `global` statement is used to specify that assignments to that name are assignments to the variable in the global (module) scope, rather than in the local scope. At the module level, this statement is redundant because the local scope and global scope are the same.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7079738Z -             markdown: '# An assert statement has a side-effect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7080847Z -               'All code defined in assert statements is ignored when optimization is requested, that is, the program is run with the `-O` flag. If an assert statement has any side-effects then the behavior of the program changes when optimization is requested.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7080938Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081020Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081150Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081320Z +               'Remove the `global` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081629Z -               'Move all expressions with side-effects out of assert statements.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081719Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081800Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7081930Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7083586Z -               'In the example, the exit code from `subprocess.call()` is checked against 0, but the entire expression is called from within an `assert` statement. If the code is ever run, then the not only the assertion itself, but also the external call, will be discarded. It is better to save the result of `subprocess.call()` to a temporary variable, and to assert that variable to be 0.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7083682Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7083896Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7084029Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7084321Z -               "assert subprocess.call(['run-backup']) == 0\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7084421Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7084521Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7084611Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7084729Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7085311Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [The global statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-global-statement).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7085515Z +             text: "# Use of 'global' at module level\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7086776Z +               'The `global` statement is used to specify that assignments to that name are assignments to the variable in the global (module) scope, rather than in the local scope. At the module level, this statement is redundant because the local scope and global scope are the same.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7087315Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The assert statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/simple_stmts.html#assert).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7087895Z -               '* TutorialsPoint, Python Programming: [Assertions in Python](http://www.tutorialspoint.com/python/assertions_in_python.htm).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7088112Z -             text: '# An assert statement has a side-effect\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7089199Z -               'All code defined in assert statements is ignored when optimization is requested, that is, the program is run with the `-O` flag. If an assert statement has any side-effects then the behavior of the program changes when optimization is requested.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7089344Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7089436Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7089562Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7089732Z +               'Remove the `global` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7090102Z -               'Move all expressions with side-effects out of assert statements.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7090185Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7090277Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7090404Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092039Z -               'In the example, the exit code from `subprocess.call()` is checked against 0, but the entire expression is called from within an `assert` statement. If the code is ever run, then the not only the assertion itself, but also the external call, will be discarded. It is better to save the result of `subprocess.call()` to a temporary variable, and to assert that variable to be 0.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092138Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092237Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092363Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092576Z -               "assert subprocess.call(['run-backup']) == 0\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092673Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092770Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7092868Z -               '\n' +
