2026-10-20T21:06:14.7170734Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7171204Z +             text: "The `__iter__` method returns a non-iterator which, if used in a 'for' loop, would raise a 'TypeError'."
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7171545Z -             text: 'Using an assert statement to test a tuple provides no validity checking.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7171623Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7171709Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7171949Z +             markdown: '# `__iter__` method returns a non-iterator\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7172243Z +               'The `__iter__` method of a class should always return an iterator.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7172415Z -             markdown: '# Asserting a tuple\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7173112Z -               'When you define an `assert` statement to test a tuple the test will either always succeed (if the tuple is non-empty) or always fail (if the tuple is empty).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7173278Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7174221Z +               'Iterators must implement both `__next__` and `__iter__` for Python 3, or both `next` and `__iter__` for Python 2. The `__iter__` method of the iterator must return the iterator object itself.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7174872Z -               'This error usually occurs when the programmer writes ` assert (condition, message) ` instead of the correct form` assert condition, message `\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7175029Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7175747Z +               'Iteration in Python relies on this behavior and attempting to iterate over an instance of a class with an incorrect `__iter__` method can raise a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7175832Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7175930Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7176055Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7176415Z +               'Make sure the value returned by `__iter__` implements the full iterator protocol.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7176739Z -               'Review the code and determine the purpose of the `assert` statement:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7176827Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7177255Z -               '* If the "tuple" has been created in error, then remove the parentheses and correct the statement\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7177837Z -               '* If validation of a tuple is intended, then you should define an `assert` statement for each element of the tuple.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7177928Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7178039Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7179344Z +               'In this example, we have implemented our own version of `range`, extending the normal functionality with the ability to skip some elements by using the `skip` method. However, the iterator `MyRangeIterator` does not fully implement the iterator protocol (namely it is missing `__iter__`).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7180050Z -               'The statement `assert (xxx, yyy)` attempts to test a "tuple" `(xxx, yyy)`. The original intention may be any of the alternatives listed below:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7180132Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7181189Z +               "Iterating over the elements in the range seems to work on the surface, for example the code `x = sum(my_range)` gives the expected result. However, if we run `sum(iter(my_range))` we get a `TypeError: 'MyRangeIterator' object is not iterable`.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7181277Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7181852Z +               'If we try to skip some elements using our custom method, for example `y = sum(my_range.skip({6,9}))`, this also raises a `TypeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7181984Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7182304Z -               'assert xxx and yyy   # Alternative 1a. Check both expressions are true\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7182386Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7182688Z +               'The fix is to implement the `__iter__` method in `MyRangeIterator`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7183081Z -               "assert xxx, yyy      # Alternative 1b. Check 'xxx' is true, 'yyy' is the failure message.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7183168Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7183571Z -               'tuple = (xxx, yyy)   # Alternative 2. Check both elements of the tuple match expectations.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7183720Z -               'assert tuple[0]==xxx\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7183987Z -               'assert tuple[1]==yyy\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7184080Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7184206Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7184357Z +               'class MyRange(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7184546Z +               '    def __init__(self, low, high):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7184686Z +               '        self.low = low\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7184921Z +               '        self.high = high\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7185023Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7185481Z -               'If you want to define a validity check on the values of a tuple then these must be tested individually.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7185569Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7185722Z +               '    def __iter__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7185951Z +               '        return MyRangeIterator(self.low, self.high)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7186038Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7186209Z +               '    def skip(self, to_skip):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7186551Z +               '        return MyRangeIterator(self.low, self.high, to_skip)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7186690Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7187296Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The assert statement](http://docs.python.org/2.7/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-assert-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7187798Z -               '* Tutorials Point: [Assertions in Python](http://www.tutorialspoint.com/python/assertions_in_python.htm).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7188213Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-670](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/670.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7188367Z -             text: '# Asserting a tuple\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7189074Z -               'When you define an `assert` statement to test a tuple the test will either always succeed (if the tuple is non-empty) or always fail (if the tuple is empty).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7189246Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7189432Z +               'class MyRangeIterator(object):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7189657Z +               '    def __init__(self, low, high, skip=None):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7189819Z +               '        self.current = low\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7190034Z +               '        self.high = high\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7190190Z +               '        self.skip = skip\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7190840Z -               'This error usually occurs when the programmer writes ` assert (condition, message) ` instead of the correct form` assert condition, message `\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7190923Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7191076Z +               '    def __next__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7191267Z +               '        if self.current >= self.high:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7191436Z +               '            raise StopIteration\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7191614Z +               '        to_return = self.current\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7191768Z +               '        self.current += 1\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7191995Z +               '        if self.skip and to_return in self.skip:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7192169Z +               '            return self.__next__()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7192316Z +               '        return to_return\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7192404Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7192635Z +               '    # Problem is fixed by uncommenting these lines\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7192783Z +               '    # def __iter__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7192930Z +               '    #     return self\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7193075Z -               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7193386Z -               'Review the code and determine the purpose of the `assert` statement:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7193473Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7193632Z +               'my_range = MyRange(0,10)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7193901Z +               'x = sum(my_range) # x = 45\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7194284Z +               "y = sum(my_range.skip({6,9})) # TypeError: 'MyRangeIterator' object is not iterable\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7194719Z -               '* If the "tuple" has been created in error, then remove the parentheses and correct the statement\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7195239Z -               '* If validation of a tuple is intended, then you should define an `assert` statement for each element of the tuple.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7195404Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7195500Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7195631Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7196262Z -               'The statement `assert (xxx, yyy)` attempts to test a "tuple" `(xxx, yyy)`. The original intention may be any of the alternatives listed below:\n' +
