2026-10-20T21:06:14.7464225Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7464967Z +             text: 'Overriding a method without ensuring that both methods accept the same number and type of parameters has the potential to cause an error when there is a mismatch.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7465528Z -             text: 'Nested loops in which the target variable is the same for each loop make the behavior of the loops difficult to understand.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7465615Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7465699Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7465952Z +             markdown: '# Signature mismatch in overriding method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7467929Z +               "When the signature of a method of a base class and a method of a subclass that overrides it don't match, a call to the base class method may not be a valid call to the subclass method, and thus raise an exception if an instance of the subclass is passed instead. If following the Liskov Substitution Principle, in which an instance of a subclass should be usable in every context as though it were an instance of the base class, this behavior breaks the principle.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7468144Z -             markdown: '# Nested loops with same variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7470011Z -               'In Python variables have function-wide scope which means that if two variables have the same name in the same scope, they are in fact one variable. Consequently, nested loops in which the target variables have the same name in fact share a single variable. Such loops are difficult to understand as the inner loop will modify the target variable of the outer loop; this may be a typographical error.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7470108Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7470198Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7470323Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7470844Z +               'Ensure that the overriding method in the subclass accepts the same parameters as the base method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7471511Z -               'Carefully examine the code and check for possible errors, particularly considering what would happen if the inner or outer variable were renamed.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7471600Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7471688Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7471799Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7472723Z +               'In the following example, `Base.runsource` takes an optional `filename` argument. However, the overriding method `Sub.runsource` does not. This means the `run` function will fail if passed an instance of `Sub`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7472812Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7472904Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7473020Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7473173Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7473309Z +               'class Base:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7473554Z +               '    def runsource(self, source, filename="<input>"):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7473682Z +               '        ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7474060Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7474174Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7474317Z +               'class Sub(Base):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7474722Z +               '    def runsource(self, source): # BAD: Does not match the signature of overridden method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7474851Z +               '        ... \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7475003Z -               'for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7475167Z -               '    for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7475299Z -               '        pass\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7475564Z -               '    print (var) # Prints 2 2 2 not 0 1 2 as might be expected\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7475656Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7475808Z +               'def run(obj: Base):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7476026Z +               '    obj.runsource("source", filename="foo.txt")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7476132Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7476226Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7476309Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7476428Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7477284Z +               '* Wikipedia: [Liskov Substitution Principle](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liskov_substitution_principle), [Method overriding](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Method_overriding#Python).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7477516Z +             text: '# Signature mismatch in overriding method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7479558Z +               "When the signature of a method of a base class and a method of a subclass that overrides it don't match, a call to the base class method may not be a valid call to the subclass method, and thus raise an exception if an instance of the subclass is passed instead. If following the Liskov Substitution Principle, in which an instance of a subclass should be usable in every context as though it were an instance of the base class, this behavior breaks the principle.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7480153Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7480731Z -               '* Python Tutorial: [for statements](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7480941Z -             text: '# Nested loops with same variable\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7482724Z -               'In Python variables have function-wide scope which means that if two variables have the same name in the same scope, they are in fact one variable. Consequently, nested loops in which the target variables have the same name in fact share a single variable. Such loops are difficult to understand as the inner loop will modify the target variable of the outer loop; this may be a typographical error.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7482872Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7482965Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7483098Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7483555Z +               'Ensure that the overriding method in the subclass accepts the same parameters as the base method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7484496Z -               'Carefully examine the code and check for possible errors, particularly considering what would happen if the inner or outer variable were renamed.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7484595Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7484677Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7484795Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7485748Z +               'In the following example, `Base.runsource` takes an optional `filename` argument. However, the overriding method `Sub.runsource` does not. This means the `run` function will fail if passed an instance of `Sub`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7485917Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486022Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486143Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486225Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486360Z +               'class Base:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486672Z +               '    def runsource(self, source, filename="<input>"):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486808Z +               '        ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7486919Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7487018Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7487172Z +               'class Sub(Base):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7487596Z +               '    def runsource(self, source): # BAD: Does not match the signature of overridden method.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7487724Z +               '        ... \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7487882Z -               'for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7488047Z -               '    for var in range(3):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7488179Z -               '        pass\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7488468Z -               '    print (var) # Prints 2 2 2 not 0 1 2 as might be expected\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7488557Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7488714Z +               'def run(obj: Base):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7488952Z +               '    obj.runsource("source", filename="foo.txt")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7489049Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7489147Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7489237Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7489354Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7490261Z +               '* Wikipedia: [Liskov Substitution Principle](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liskov_substitution_principle), [Method overriding](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Method_overriding#Python).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7490880Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7491386Z -               '* Python Tutorial: [for statements](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements).\n'
