2026-10-20T21:06:14.7588192Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7588281Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7588567Z +             markdown: '# Non-standard exception raised in special method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7590400Z +               'User-defined classes interact with the Python virtual machine via special methods (also called "magic methods"). For example, for a class to support addition it must implement the `__add__` and `__radd__` special methods. When the expression `a + b` is evaluated, the Python virtual machine will call `type(a).__add__(a, b)`, and if that is not implemented it will call `type(b).__radd__(b, a)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7590814Z -             markdown: '# Modification of dictionary returned by locals()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7591870Z -               "The dictionary returned by `locals()` is not a view of the function's locals, but a copy. Therefore, modification of the dictionary returned from `locals()` will not modify the local variables of the function.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7591961Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7594206Z +               'Since the virtual machine calls these special methods for common expressions, users of the class will expect these operations to raise standard exceptions. For example, users would expect that the expression `a.b` may raise an `AttributeError` if the object `a` does not have an attribute `b`. If a `KeyError` were raised instead, then this would be unexpected and may break code that expected an `AttributeError`, but not a `KeyError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7594311Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7594997Z +               'Therefore, if a method is unable to perform the expected operation then its response should conform to the standard protocol, described below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7595100Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7595496Z +               '* Attribute access, `a.b` (`__getattr__`): Raise `AttributeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7595982Z +               '* Arithmetic operations, `a + b` (`__add__`): Do not raise an exception, return `NotImplemented` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7596298Z +               '* Indexing, `a[b]` (`__getitem__`): Raise `KeyError` or `IndexError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7597142Z +               '* Hashing, `hash(a)` (`__hash__`): Should not raise an exception. Use `__hash__ = None` to indicate that an object is unhashable rather than raising an exception.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7597600Z +               '* Equality methods, `a == b` (`__eq__`): Never raise an exception, always return `True` or `False`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7598055Z +               '* Ordering comparison methods, `a < b` (`__lt__`): Raise a `TypeError` if the objects cannot be ordered.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7598733Z +               '* Most others: If the operation is never supported, the method often does not need to be implemented at all; otherwise a `TypeError` should be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7598829Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7598963Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7600216Z +               'If the method always raises as exception, then if it is intended to be an abstract method, the `@abstractmethod` decorator should be used. Otherwise, ensure that the method raises an exception of the correct type, or remove the method if the operation does not need to be supported.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7600525Z -               'If the intention is to modify a local variable, then do so directly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7600614Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7600700Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7600812Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7602000Z +               'In the following example, the `__add__` method of `A` raises a `TypeError` if `other` is of the wrong type. However, it should return `NotImplemented` instead of rising an exception, to allow other classes to support adding to `A`. This is demonstrated in the class `B`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7602547Z -               'In this example, rather than assigning to the variable `z` directly, the dictionary returned by `locals()` is modified.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7602630Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7602719Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7602834Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7603028Z +               'class A:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7603197Z +               '    def __init__(self, a):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7603342Z +               '        self.a = a \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7603423Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7603595Z +               '    def __add__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7603990Z +               '        # BAD: Should return NotImplemented instead of raising\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7604183Z +               '        if not isinstance(other,A):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7604462Z +               '            raise TypeError(f"Cannot add A to {other.__class__}")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7604710Z +               '        return A(self.a + other.a)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7604892Z -               'def modifies_locals_sum(x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7605053Z -               "    locals()['z'] = x + y\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7605454Z -               '    #z will not be defined as modifications to locals() do not alter the local variables.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7605593Z -               '    return z\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7605682Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7605807Z +               'class B:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7605966Z +               '    def __init__(self, a):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7606109Z +               '        self.a = a \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7606253Z -               'def fixed_sum(x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7606383Z -               '    z = x + y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7606510Z -               '    return z\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7606599Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7606837Z +               '    def __add__(self, other):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7607520Z +               '        # GOOD: Returning NotImplemented allows for the operation to fallback to other implementations to allow other classes to support adding to B.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7607768Z +               '        if not isinstance(other,B):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7607950Z +               '            return NotImplemented\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7608129Z +               '        return B(self.a + other.a)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7608232Z +               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7608317Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7608437Z +               '        \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7608539Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7608625Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7609229Z +               'In the following example, the `__getitem__` method of `C` raises a `ValueError`, rather than a `KeyError` or `IndexError` as expected.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7609315Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7609450Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7610038Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7610524Z -               '* Python Tutorial: [for statements](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7610785Z -             text: '# Modification of dictionary returned by locals()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7611719Z -               "The dictionary returned by `locals()` is not a view of the function's locals, but a copy. Therefore, modification of the dictionary returned from `locals()` will not modify the local variables of the function.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7611808Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7611932Z +               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7612056Z +               'class C:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7612231Z +               '    def __getitem__(self, idx):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7612390Z +               '        if self.idx < 0:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7612666Z +               '            # BAD: Should raise a KeyError or IndexError instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7612874Z +               '            raise ValueError("Invalid index")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7613044Z +               '        return self.lookup(idx)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7613144Z +               ' \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7613300Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7613403Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7614747Z +               'In the following example, the class `__hash__` method of `D` raises `NotImplementedError`. This causes `D` to be incorrectly identified as hashable by `isinstance(obj, collections.abc.Hashable)`; so the correct way to make a class unhashable is to set `__hash__ = None`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7614892Z -               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7615202Z -               'If the intention is to modify a local variable, then do so directly.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7615291Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7615452Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7615592Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616140Z -               'In this example, rather than assigning to the variable `z` directly, the dictionary returned by `locals()` is modified.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616236Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616337Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616448Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616568Z +               'class D:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616725Z +               '    def __hash__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7616927Z +               '        # BAD: Use `__hash__ = None` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7617246Z +               '        raise NotImplementedError(f"{self.__class__} is unhashable.")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7617350Z +               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7617431Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7617565Z +               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7618496Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [Special Method Names](http://docs.python.org/dev/reference/datamodel.html#special-method-names).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7619093Z +               '* Python Library Reference: [Exceptions](https://docs.python.org/3/library/exceptions.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7619462Z +             text: '# Non-standard exception raised in special method\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7622604Z +               'User-defined classes interact with the Python virtual machine via special methods (also called "magic methods"). For example, for a class to support addition it must implement the `__add__` and `__radd__` special methods. When the expression `a + b` is evaluated, the Python virtual machine will call `type(a).__add__(a, b)`, and if that is not implemented it will call `type(b).__radd__(b, a)`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7622893Z -               'def modifies_locals_sum(x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7623205Z -               "    locals()['z'] = x + y\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7623653Z -               '    #z will not be defined as modifications to locals() do not alter the local variables.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7623986Z -               '    return z\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7624167Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7626783Z +               'Since the virtual machine calls these special methods for common expressions, users of the class will expect these operations to raise standard exceptions. For example, users would expect that the expression `a.b` may raise an `AttributeError` if the object `a` does not have an attribute `b`. If a `KeyError` were raised instead, then this would be unexpected and may break code that expected an `AttributeError`, but not a `KeyError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7627094Z -               'def fixed_sum(x, y):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7627241Z -               '    z = x + y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7627451Z -               '    return z\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7627607Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7628481Z +               'Therefore, if a method is unable to perform the expected operation then its response should conform to the standard protocol, described below.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7628595Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7628911Z +               '* Attribute access, `a.b` (`__getattr__`): Raise `AttributeError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7629459Z +               '* Arithmetic operations, `a + b` (`__add__`): Do not raise an exception, return `NotImplemented` instead.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7630192Z +               '* Indexing, `a[b]` (`__getitem__`): Raise `KeyError` or `IndexError`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7631357Z +               '* Hashing, `hash(a)` (`__hash__`): Should not raise an exception. Use `__hash__ = None` to indicate that an object is unhashable rather than raising an exception.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7632092Z +               '* Equality methods, `a == b` (`__eq__`): Never raise an exception, always return `True` or `False`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7632999Z +               '* Ordering comparison methods, `a < b` (`__lt__`): Raise a `TypeError` if the objects cannot be ordered.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7634356Z +               '* Most others: If the operation is never supported, the method often does not need to be implemented at all; otherwise a `TypeError` should be raised.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7634537Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7634680Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7634889Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7635828Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The for statement](http://docs.python.org/2/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-for-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7636595Z -               '* Python Tutorial: [for statements](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/controlflow.html#for-statements).\n'
