2026-10-20T21:06:14.7793392Z -           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7793521Z -           fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7794309Z -             text: "Using a 'try-finally' block to ensure only that a resource is closed makes code more difficult to read."
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7794407Z -           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7794503Z -           help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7794717Z -             markdown: "# Should use a 'with' statement\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7795490Z -               'The `with` statement was introduced by PEP343 to allow standard uses of `try-finally` statements to be factored out. Using this simplification makes code easier to read.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7795575Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7795662Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7795787Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7796409Z +               'In order to ensure correct cleanup of the object add an explicit close(), or similar, method. Possibly make the object a context manager.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7797482Z -               'Review the code and determine whether or not the `try-finally` is used only to ensure that a resource is closed. If the only purpose is to ensure that a resource is closed, then replace the `try-finally` statement with a `with` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7797680Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7797901Z +               'The __del__ method should just call close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7797990Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7798088Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7798202Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7799050Z +               'The first example below shows a class which relies on `__del__` to release resources. The second example shows an improved version of the class where `__del__` simply calls close.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800149Z -               'The following code shows examples of different ways of ensuring that a file is always closed, even when an error is generated. In the second example, the `try-finally` block is replaced by a simpler `with` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800239Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800326Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800439Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800529Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800810Z +               '#Relies on __del__ being called by the garbage collector.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7800989Z +               'class CachedPreferencesFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7801145Z -               'f = open("filename")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7801407Z -               'try:                         # Method of ensuring file closure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7801545Z -               '    f.write(...)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7801677Z -               'finally:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7801882Z -               '    f.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7801968Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7802080Z +               '    ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7802163Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7802319Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7802631Z +               '        for key, value in self.preferences.items():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7802827Z +               '            self.write_pair(key, value)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7803005Z +               '        self.backing.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7803343Z -               'with open("filename") as f:  # Simpler method of ensuring file closure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7803481Z -               '    f.write(...)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7803586Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7803677Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7804024Z -               '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7804657Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The with statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-with-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7805229Z -               '* Python Standard Library: [Context manager ](http://docs.python.org/library/stdtypes.html#context-manager-types).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7805618Z -               '* Python PEP 343: [The "with" Statement](http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0343).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7805828Z -             text: "# Should use a 'with' statement\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7806645Z -               'The `with` statement was introduced by PEP343 to allow standard uses of `try-finally` statements to be factored out. Using this simplification makes code easier to read.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7806731Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7806879Z +               '#Better version\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7807054Z +               'class CachedPreferencesFile\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7807148Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7807256Z +               '    ...\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7807401Z -               '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7808666Z -               'Review the code and determine whether or not the `try-finally` is used only to ensure that a resource is closed. If the only purpose is to ensure that a resource is closed, then replace the `try-finally` statement with a `with` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7808768Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7808920Z +               '    def close(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7809269Z +               '        for key, value in self.preferences.items():\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7809470Z +               '            self.write_pair(key, value)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7809639Z +               '        self.backing.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7809732Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7809890Z +               '    def __del__(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7810036Z +               '        self.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7810175Z -               '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7811263Z -               'The following code shows examples of different ways of ensuring that a file is always closed, even when an error is generated. In the second example, the `try-finally` block is replaced by a simpler `with` statement.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7811359Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7811465Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7811592Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7811700Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7811853Z -               'f = open("filename")\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7812112Z -               'try:                         # Method of ensuring file closure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7812258Z -               '    f.write(...)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7812392Z -               'finally:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7812526Z -               '    f.close()\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7812627Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7812723Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7813060Z -               'with open("filename") as f:  # Simpler method of ensuring file closure\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7813270Z -               '    f.write(...)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7813363Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7813454Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7813577Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7814463Z +               '* Python Standard Library: [Context manager](http://docs.python.org/library/stdtypes.html#context-manager-types).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7815102Z -               '* Python Language Reference: [The with statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-with-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7815671Z -               '* Python Standard Library: [Context manager ](http://docs.python.org/library/stdtypes.html#context-manager-types).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816061Z -               '* Python PEP 343: [The "with" Statement](http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0343).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816154Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816330Z +           id: 'py/overly-complex-delete',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816504Z +           name: 'py/overly-complex-delete',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816660Z -           id: 'py/should-use-with',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816811Z -           name: 'py/should-use-with',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7816914Z             properties: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7817081Z               'problem.severity': 'recommendation',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7817222Z +             'sub-severity': 'low',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7817778Z +             description: '`__del__` methods may be called at arbitrary times, perhaps never called at all, and should be simple.',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7817948Z +             id: 'py/overly-complex-delete',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7818090Z -             'sub-severity': 'high',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7818564Z -             description: "Using a 'try-finally' block to ensure only that a resource is closed makes code more\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7818741Z -               '              difficult to read.',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7818889Z -             id: 'py/should-use-with',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819012Z               kind: 'problem',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819202Z +             name: 'Overly complex `__del__` method',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819335Z +             precision: 'high',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819520Z -             name: "Should use a 'with' statement",
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819663Z -             precision: 'very-high',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819753Z               tags: [
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7819849Z                 'quality',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820057Z                 'maintainability',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820174Z +               'complexity'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820287Z -               'readability'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820368Z               ]
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820454Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820573Z             shortDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820759Z +             text: 'Overly complex `__del__` method'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7820938Z -             text: "Should use a 'with' statement"
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821018Z             }
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821106Z           },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821192Z           {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821423Z             defaultConfiguration: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821523Z               enabled: true,
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821635Z +             level: 'error'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821763Z -             level: 'warning'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821852Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7821969Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7822349Z +             text: 'Modifying the default value of a parameter can lead to unexpected results.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7822595Z -             text: 'The conditional is always true or always false'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7822676Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7822768Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7823025Z +             markdown: '# Modification of parameter with default\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7825527Z +               'The default value of a parameter is computed once when the function is created, not for every invocation. The "pre-computed" value is then used for every subsequent call to the function. Consequently, if you modify the default value for a parameter this "modified" default value is used for the parameter in future calls to the function. This means that the function may not behave as expected in future calls and also makes the function more difficult to understand.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7825901Z -             markdown: '# Constant in conditional expression or statement\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7826659Z -               'Using a constant value as a test in a conditional statement renders the statement pointless as only one branch will be run regardless of any other factors.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7826747Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7826839Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7826972Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7828674Z +               'If a parameter has a default value, do not modify the default value. When you use a mutable object as a default value, you should use a placeholder value instead of modifying the default value. This is a particular problem when you work with lists and dictionaries but there are standard methods of avoiding modifying the default parameter (see References).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7829497Z -               'If the conditional statement is required for debugging or similar then use a variable instead. Otherwise, remove the conditional statement and any associated dead code.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7829591Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7829675Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7829797Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7831018Z +               'In the following example, the `default` parameter is set with a default value of an empty list. Other commands in the function then append values to the list. The next time the function is called, the list will contain values, which may not have been intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7831715Z -               'In the first example the `if` statement will always be executed and therefore can be removed. The contents of the statement should be kept though.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7831801Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7832986Z -               "In the second example the statement `l = 100` is never executed because `1 > 100` is always false. However, it is likely that the intention was `l > 100` (the number '1' being misread as the letter 'l') and that the test should be corrected, rather than deleted.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7833146Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7833242Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7833357Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7833483Z -               'if True:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7833658Z -               '    print "True is true!"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7833746Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7834359Z +               '    def __init__(self, name, choices=[], default=[], shortDesc=None,\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7834809Z +               "                 longDesc=None, hints=None, allowNone=1):   # 'default' parameter assigned a value\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7835004Z +               '        self.choices = choices\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7835316Z +               '        if choices and not default:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7835755Z +               "            default.append(choices[0][1])                   # value of 'default' parameter modified\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7836205Z +               '        Argument.__init__(self, name, default, shortDesc, longDesc, hints, allowNone=allowNone)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7836347Z -               'def limit(l):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7836492Z -               '    if l < -100:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7836633Z -               '        l = -100\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7836766Z -               '    if 1 > 100:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7836900Z -               '        l = 100\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7837036Z -               '    return l\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7837136Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7837229Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7838197Z +               'The recommended workaround is use a placeholder value. That is, define the function with a default of `default=None`, check if the parameter is `None` and then set the parameter to a list.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7838293Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7838462Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7838579Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7839249Z +               '* Effbot: [Default Parameter Values in Python](https://web.archive.org/web/20201112004749/http://effbot.org/zone/default-values.htm).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7839897Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [Function definitions](http://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#function-definitions).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7840129Z +             text: '# Modification of parameter with default\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7842328Z +               'The default value of a parameter is computed once when the function is created, not for every invocation. The "pre-computed" value is then used for every subsequent call to the function. Consequently, if you modify the default value for a parameter this "modified" default value is used for the parameter in future calls to the function. This means that the function may not behave as expected in future calls and also makes the function more difficult to understand.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7842827Z -               '* Python: [The If Statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-if-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7843346Z -               '* Python: [The While Statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-while-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7844048Z -               '* Python: [Literals (constant values)](http://docs.python.org/reference/expressions.html#literals).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7844505Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-561](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/561.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7844941Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-570](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/570.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7845374Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-571](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/571.html).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7845644Z -             text: '# Constant in conditional expression or statement\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7846386Z -               'Using a constant value as a test in a conditional statement renders the statement pointless as only one branch will be run regardless of any other factors.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7846565Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7846655Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7846783Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7848496Z +               'If a parameter has a default value, do not modify the default value. When you use a mutable object as a default value, you should use a placeholder value instead of modifying the default value. This is a particular problem when you work with lists and dictionaries but there are standard methods of avoiding modifying the default parameter (see References).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7849378Z -               'If the conditional statement is required for debugging or similar then use a variable instead. Otherwise, remove the conditional statement and any associated dead code.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7849474Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7849558Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7849675Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7850895Z +               'In the following example, the `default` parameter is set with a default value of an empty list. Other commands in the function then append values to the list. The next time the function is called, the list will contain values, which may not have been intended.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7851585Z -               'In the first example the `if` statement will always be executed and therefore can be removed. The contents of the statement should be kept though.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7851673Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7852918Z -               "In the second example the statement `l = 100` is never executed because `1 > 100` is always false. However, it is likely that the intention was `l > 100` (the number '1' being misread as the letter 'l') and that the test should be corrected, rather than deleted.\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7853061Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7853161Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7853280Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7853404Z -               'if True:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7853573Z -               '    print "True is true!"\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7853655Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7854233Z +               '    def __init__(self, name, choices=[], default=[], shortDesc=None,\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7854677Z +               "                 longDesc=None, hints=None, allowNone=1):   # 'default' parameter assigned a value\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7854854Z +               '        self.choices = choices\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7855046Z +               '        if choices and not default:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7855466Z +               "            default.append(choices[0][1])                   # value of 'default' parameter modified\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7855903Z +               '        Argument.__init__(self, name, default, shortDesc, longDesc, hints, allowNone=allowNone)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856047Z -               'def limit(l):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856197Z -               '    if l < -100:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856335Z -               '        l = -100\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856474Z -               '    if 1 > 100:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856609Z -               '        l = 100\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856738Z -               '    return l\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856836Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7856922Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7857822Z +               'The recommended workaround is use a placeholder value. That is, define the function with a default of `default=None`, check if the parameter is `None` and then set the parameter to a list.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7857916Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7858010Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7858133Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7858767Z +               '* Effbot: [Default Parameter Values in Python](https://web.archive.org/web/20201112004749/http://effbot.org/zone/default-values.htm).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7859461Z +               '* Python Language Reference: [Function definitions](http://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#function-definitions).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7859930Z -               '* Python: [The If Statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-if-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7860431Z -               '* Python: [The While Statement](http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-while-statement).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7860887Z -               '* Python: [Literals (constant values)](http://docs.python.org/reference/expressions.html#literals).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7861373Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-561](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/561.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7861793Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-570](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/570.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7862197Z -               '* Common Weakness Enumeration: [CWE-571](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/571.html).\n'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7862285Z             },
