2026-10-20T21:06:14.7868441Z ...
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7868551Z             fullDescription: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7868989Z +             text: 'By the PEP8 style guide, the first parameter of a normal method should be named `self`.'
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7869529Z -             text: "Using 'assertTrue' or 'assertFalse' rather than a more specific assertion can give uninformative failure messages."
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7869684Z             },
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7869771Z             help: {
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7870052Z +             markdown: "# First parameter of a method is not named 'self'\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7870500Z +               'Normal methods should have at least one parameter and the first parameter should be called `self`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7870672Z -             markdown: '# Imprecise assert\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7872496Z -               'The class `unittest.TestCase` provides a range of assertion methods. As well as the general forms `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` more specific forms such as `assertGreaterEquals()` and `assertNotIn()` are provided. By using the more specific forms it is possible to get more precise and informative failure messages in the event of a test failing. This can speed up the debugging process.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7872585Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7872677Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7872799Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7873321Z +               'Ensure that the first parameter of a normal method is named `self`, as recommended by the style guidelines in PEP 8.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7874516Z -               'Replace all calls to `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` that do not provide a custom failure message with a more specific variant. Alternatively, provide a tailored failure message using the `assertTrue(condition, message)` form.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7874607Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7875289Z +               'If a `self` parameter is unneeded, the method should be decorated with `staticmethod`, or moved out of the class as a regular function.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7875381Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7875542Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7875653Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7876299Z +               'In the following cases, the first argument of `Point.__init__` is named `val` instead; whereas in `Point2.__init__` it is correctly named `self`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7876591Z -               'In this example, `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` are used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7876672Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7876756Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7876862Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7876998Z +               'class Point:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7877305Z +               "    def __init__(val, x, y):  # BAD: first parameter is mis-named 'val'\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7877445Z +               '        val._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7877585Z +               '        val._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7877760Z -               'from unittest import TestCase\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7877840Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7877979Z +               'class Point2:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7878326Z +               "    def __init__(self, x, y):  # GOOD: first parameter is correctly named 'self'\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7878462Z +               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7878597Z +               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7878753Z -               'class MyTest(TestCase):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7878855Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7878955Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7879103Z -               '    def testInts(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7879281Z -               '        self.assertTrue(1 == 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7879445Z -               '        self.assertFalse(1 > 2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7879663Z -               '        self.assertTrue(1 in []) #This will fail\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7879759Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7879848Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7880638Z -               'This will make it more difficult to determine what has gone wrong when `self.assertTrue(1 in [])` fails. The failure message "AssertionError: False is not true" is not very helpful.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7880801Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7881348Z -               'A more useful error message can be generated by changing the asserts to the more specific forms as in the following example.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7881439Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7881538Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7881663Z -               '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7881837Z -               'from unittest import TestCase\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7881933Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7882081Z -               'class MyTest(TestCase):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7882182Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7882344Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7882494Z -               '    def testInts(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7882665Z -               '        self.assertEqual(1, 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7882845Z -               '        self.assertLessEqual(1, 2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7883054Z -               '        self.assertIn(1, []) #This will fail\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7883151Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7883244Z -               '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7884395Z -               'In this case, the failure message "AssertionError: 1 not found in \\[\\]" is much more informative.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7884594Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7884764Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7884978Z                 '## References\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7886086Z +               '* Python PEP 8: [Function and method arguments](http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0008/#function-and-method-arguments).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7886481Z +               '* Python Tutorial: [Classes](http://docs.python.org/2/tutorial/classes.html).\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7886918Z +               '* Python Docs: [staticmethod](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#staticmethod).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7887252Z +             text: "# First parameter of a method is not named 'self'\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7887699Z +               'Normal methods should have at least one parameter and the first parameter should be called `self`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7888305Z -               '* Python library reference: [TestCase.assertEqual](https://docs.python.org/library/unittest.html#unittest.TestCase.assertEqual).\n',
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7888465Z -             text: '# Imprecise assert\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7890156Z -               'The class `unittest.TestCase` provides a range of assertion methods. As well as the general forms `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` more specific forms such as `assertGreaterEquals()` and `assertNotIn()` are provided. By using the more specific forms it is possible to get more precise and informative failure messages in the event of a test failing. This can speed up the debugging process.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7890247Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7890334Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7890454Z                 '## Recommendation\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7890974Z +               'Ensure that the first parameter of a normal method is named `self`, as recommended by the style guidelines in PEP 8.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7891981Z -               'Replace all calls to `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` that do not provide a custom failure message with a more specific variant. Alternatively, provide a tailored failure message using the `assertTrue(condition, message)` form.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7892063Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7892656Z +               'If a `self` parameter is unneeded, the method should be decorated with `staticmethod`, or moved out of the class as a regular function.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7892746Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7892836Z +               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7892952Z                 '## Example\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7893585Z +               'In the following cases, the first argument of `Point.__init__` is named `val` instead; whereas in `Point2.__init__` it is correctly named `self`.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894088Z -               'In this example, `assertTrue()` and `assertFalse()` are used.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894181Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894269Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894376Z                 '```python\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894513Z +               'class Point:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894817Z +               "    def __init__(val, x, y):  # BAD: first parameter is mis-named 'val'\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7894959Z +               '        val._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7895100Z +               '        val._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7895346Z -               'from unittest import TestCase\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7895437Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7895573Z +               'class Point2:\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7895905Z +               "    def __init__(self, x, y):  # GOOD: first parameter is correctly named 'self'\n" +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896048Z +               '        self._x = x\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896188Z +               '        self._y = y\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896344Z -               'class MyTest(TestCase):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896447Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896541Z -               '    \n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896695Z -               '    def testInts(self):\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7896869Z -               '        self.assertTrue(1 == 1)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7897032Z -               '        self.assertFalse(1 > 2)\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7897254Z -               '        self.assertTrue(1 in []) #This will fail\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7897422Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7897511Z                 '```\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7898315Z -               'This will make it more difficult to determine what has gone wrong when `self.assertTrue(1 in [])` fails. The failure message "AssertionError: False is not true" is not very helpful.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7898465Z                 '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7899010Z -               'A more useful error message can be generated by changing the asserts to the more specific forms as in the following example.\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7899108Z -               '\n' +
2026-10-20T21:06:14.7899198Z -               '\n' +
